    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.modular_product - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.modular_product.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.modular_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the modular product of this graph with another.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.modular_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the modular product of this graph with another.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.modular_product()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute the modular product of this graph with another.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the modular product of this graph with another.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L52094-L52176){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.modular_product(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Modular product](../assets/graphs/modular-product.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G is a paw: a triangle A, B, C with a tail C–D. H is a 4-cycle 1, 2, 3, 4. The path D–C–B in G and the path 1–2–3 in H (accent; the rest muted) are the same induced subgraph. Their modular product has 16 nodes and 40 edges, and its largest clique, drawn on the right as D1, C2, B3 for the pairs (D, 1), (C, 2), (B, 3), has 3 nodes: the largest induced subgraph common to both graphs.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe modular product has a node for every pair `(g, h)` and joins `(g, h)` to\n`(g', h')` (with `g != g'` and `h != h'`) when the two pairs \"agree\": `g`-`g'` and\n`h`-`h'` are both edges, or both non-edges. Its cliques correspond exactly to induced\nsubgraphs common to both graphs, so finding a maximum clique in the modular product\nsolves the maximum common induced subgraph problem, a staple of comparing molecules\nand other structures (see `maximum_clique()`).\n\nBoth graphs must be undirected. The result is simple and unweighted (parallel edges in\nthe factors are collapsed), with the same node numbering and labels as\n`cartesian_product()`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The second factor. Neither graph is modified.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new undirected graph with `n * m` nodes.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If either graph is directed.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nEvery pair of pairs is checked, $O(n^2 m^2)$ time, so the method suits small graphs, as\nthe maximum common subgraph problem itself is NP-hard. A clique $\\{(g_1, h_1), \\ldots,\n(g_k, h_k)\\}$ maps $g_i \\mapsto h_i$ isomorphically between the induced subgraphs\n(Barrow and Burstall, 1976). Matches NetworkX's `modular_product`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~maximum_clique`) \n\n:   Find a largest clique.\n\n[](`~tensor_product`) \n\n:   Join pairs that move in both graphs at once.\n\n[](`~is_isomorphic`) \n\n:   Check whether two graphs are isomorphic.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nTwo pairs `(a, b)` and `(c, d)` are joined when `a--c` and `b--d` are both edges or both\nnon-edges. A clique in the product matches nodes of the two graphs with the same edges\namong them, so the largest clique gives the largest induced subgraph the graphs share.\n\n### A triangle with a tail, inside a square\n\nA triangle with a tail and a 4-cycle share no triangle, but they share a path of three\nnodes. The product's largest clique has 3 nodes, and its pairs say which nodes match:\n\n::: {#b8b72832 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\npaw = ec.Graph().add_nodes([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"])\npaw.add_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\"), (\"C\", \"D\")])\nsquare = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(4)\n\nproduct = paw.modular_product(square)\nclique = product.maximum_clique()\n\nsorted(product.get_label_from_id(n) for n in clique)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['(B, 3)', '(C, 2)', '(D, 1)']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n`B--C--D` in the first graph matches `3--2--1` in the square. In the drawing of the product,\nthe clique is highlighted:\n\n::: {#14915a5d .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nproduct.plot(\n    label_by=\"label\", highlight=clique, seed=1,\n    highlight_edges=[(u, v) for u in clique for v in clique if u < v],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-5